  "name": "Question 5",
  "problem": "Solve the inequality $\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot {3}^{2x-1}\\ge 2.$\n\n$x$\n{{field-1}}{{field-2}}",
  "worked_solution": "Isolating the exponential term, we find\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot {3}^{2x-1} & \\ge 2 \\\\ {3}^{2x-1} & \\ge 8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, taking the logarithm to base $3$ of both sides and applying the laws of logarithms, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{log}_{3}\u2061({3}^{2x-1}) & \\ge {log}_{3}\u2061(8) \\\\ 2x-1 & \\ge {log}_{3}\u2061(8) \\\\ 2x & \\ge {log}_{3}\u2061(8)+1 \\\\ x & \\ge \\frac{1}{2}({log}_{3}\u2061(8)+1).\\end{aligned}\n$$",
